[1.2 Institutional Framework for Public Debt Management]

The Public Finance Management (PFM) Act, 2012, Section 62 (1) (2) (3) and Section 63 provide for  the  establishment  of  the  Public  Debt  Management  Office  (PDMO)  within  the  National Treasury.  In  executing  its  functions,  the  PDMO  is  organised  into  three  departments  namely: Resource Mobilization Department (Front Office); Debt Policy, Strategy and Risk Management Department (Middle Office); and, the Debt Recording and Settlement Department (Back Office). The functions of each department are distinct, but inter-related calling for a collaboration in the execution of the overall mandate of the Directorate.

Functions of the Public Debt Management Office include:

Carrying out the government's debt management policy aimed at minimising its costs over the long-term taking account of risks;

Maintaining a reliable debt register for all loans taken by the national government including loans guaranteed by the national government;

Preparing and updating the annual medium-term debt management strategy including debt sustainability analysis;

Preparing and implementing the national government borrowing plan including servicing of outstanding debt;

Acting as  the  principal  in  the  issuance  of  Government  debt  securities  on  behalf  of  the National Treasury;

Monitor and evaluate all borrowing and debt-related transactions to ensure that they are within the guidelines and risk parameters of the public debt management strategy;

Process the issuance of loan guarantees including assessment and management of risks in national government guarantees;

Transact in derivative financial instruments in accordance with international best practices benchmarked; and

Ensure management operations are supported by an accurate, reliable and comprehensive management information system with proper safeguards and controlled roles to specified users.

[Key Result Area 1 : Strengthen PDMO organizational structure and operational capability]

The objectives include:

Consolidation  of  core  debt  functions  [borrowing,  recording  and  settlement;  policy  and strategy] under the respective departments;

Preparation of workflow plans and manuals for business operations;

Staff capacity building through establishment of a PDMO capacity building project.

[Achievements]

The Directorate was able to undertake the following debt service processes:

Debt Ledgers were digitally recorded and uploaded in treasury servers;

End User Licensing Agreement (EULA) for the Commonwealth Meridian was signed;

Commonwealth Meridian Demo for IFMIS Interface was undertaken;

Data migration from CS-CDRMS to Commonwealth Meridian achieved;

Digitization of all loan records including loan agreements, legal opinion and other statutory documents; and

Parallel run of CS-CDRMS and Commonwealth Meridian.

[Recommendations]

Full stabilization of the Commonwealth Meridian System;

Integration of debt management system (Meridian) with IFMIS and Internet Banking (IB);

Debt  data  reconciliation  with  creditors  to  be  undertaken  and  reviewed  quarterly  to improve accuracy of debt reports; and

Training of PDMO staff and other stakeholders on usage of Commonwealth Meridian system.

[Recommendations]

Full participation of staff during budget preparation recommended;

Ensure provision of GOK counterpart funds for donor funded projects in the financing agreements  and  also  ensure  provision  of  additional  conditional  grants  for  the  projects implemented in the Counties;

Need  for  more  collaboration  with  the  Budget  Department  and  the  Macro  Economic Working Group;

Joint Portfolio performance reviews, supervision missions, fiduciary clinics and field visits to be held to review the project implementation status, disbursement levels, financial and procurement management of projects funded by the Development Partners; and

Meetings to be held regularly with major stakeholders to discuss policy issues affecting donor funded projects such as Tax exemptions, availability of GOK counterpart funds and low absorption of donor funded projects.

[Key Result Area 5: Broaden and deepen the domestic debt market]

Auction  Management  Rules  and  Guidelines  published  on  the  Central  Bank  of  Kenya (CBK) website;

The  Medium-Term  Debt  Management  Strategy  (MTDS)  has  provided  clarity  on government funding strategy enhancing investor confidence in the domestic debt market;

PDMO has closely collaborated with the Central Bank of Kenya (CBK) through regular consultative  forums with relevant stakeholders to support successful issuance of government securities; and

[Recommendations]

Operationalize the Over-The-Counter Trading for Treasury Bonds to improve liquidity;

Reengineering of retail bonds issuance to promote a saving culture and enhance financial inclusion;

Build staff capacity in the IRU; and

The IRU microsite should be updated.

The Investor Relations Unit (IRU) to be revamped to provided transparent and accurate data on the government debt and other relevant information to stakeholders.

[Key Result Area 7: Restructure the debt portfolio to minimize risks and costs]

The primary objective of public debt management is to borrow at the optimal cost and at a prudent degree of risk;

Over the period under review, PDMO successfully applied for concessional financing from the  World Bank under Development Policy Operations program, African Development Bank (ADB) and IMF budget support. This strategic initiative led to significant increase in the composition of concessional debt in the public debt portfolio;

Fiscal consolidation measures adopted in order to reduce the fiscal deficits to GDP ratio to a projected 3.6% in the FY 2025/26; and

Focus on the refinancing the 2024 International Sovereign Bond maturity.

[7.1 Assumption of Projections]

The projections were based on the following assumptions:

Government Overdraft facility was to be utilized during the year, but would be repaid and close at zero outstanding balance at the end of the financial year;

The Treasury bonds and bills interest rates were assumed to remain constant at prevailing rates of 8.0 percent for 91 days, 9.2 percent for 182 days and 9.9 percent for 364 days

Treasury bills. The Treasury bonds issuances were to be benchmark bonds of 2, 5, 10, 15 and 25 years with yields ranging between 11.3 percent and 13.9 percent per annum;

Assumed exchange rate variations due to the depreciation of the Kenyan shilling against major foreign currencies within the specified period resulting from worldwide instabilities including global inflation and geopolitical tension;

Deficit financing and other targeted Commercial debts in the portfolio would be successful;

The out-turn as at end June 2023 indicates a relatively stable domestic interest rates trend with gradual increase due to external shocks; and

Domestic debt interest payments of Ksh 533,019 million due to decreased uptake of both treasury bills and bonds in the domestic debt market.

